thought
<channel|>We love the convenience of apps like Libby, but "free" for readers doesn't mean free for the library. Every digital checkout carries a hidden maintenance cost. Unlike physical books that sit on shelves for decades, digital licenses are often ephemeral—expiring after a set number of borrows or a specific timeframe. This forces libraries to repeatedly repurchase the same titles just to keep their digital shelves stocked. It creates a relentless cycle of spending that strains budgets already stretched thin between physical collections and vital community programs. [end of text]


